---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You are assigned a district and a save file in week one, and you keep it
    for the whole semester. Each week takes one planning problem — zoning,
    transit, infrastructure, budget, the public realm — and you make the call
    in Cities: Skylines II, then defend it at crit against three named
    metrics: fiscal performance, mobility performance and livability. No
    build gets all three. Learning to say which one you traded, and why, is
    the actual syllabus. You leave with a comprehensive plan for a district
    you can walk a critic through, screenshot by screenshot.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Second-year students who've done a first-year planning or urban studies
    gateway course and want their first studio — the crit format, the weekly
    deliverable, the discipline of defending a design decision out loud. No
    prior experience with the software is assumed; the studio teaches the
    tool alongside the theory. If you want a semester of debate about whether
    cities should exist at all, this isn't that course — every week here ends
    with something built.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each studio.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
